<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s08e01" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 1: Solving Inequalities, Part 1</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 1 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Interpret inequality notation.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Solve simple inequalities.  
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 1 Terminology</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Inequality
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Compound Inequality 
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 1 Key Symbols</title>
    <p>
      <md>
        \ge \quad\quad \text{as in}\,\, A\ge 2000
      </md>
      <md>
        \le \quad\quad \text{as in}\,\, T\le 32
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    The symbol <m>\ge</m> “greater than or equal to.” Think of it as a fusion of the <m>\gt</m> symbol and the <m>=</m> symbol.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, suppose the variable A represents our income, and we have $2000 worth of bills to pay. 
    Then our income needs to be greater than or equal to $2000, enough money to pay our bills and hopefully a little more:
    <md>
      A\ge 2000
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    The symbol <m>\le</m> “greater than or equal to.” Think of it as a fusion of the <m>\lt</m> symbol and the <m>=</m> symbol.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, suppose the variable T represents the temperature of ice (in degrees Fahrenheit). 
    As long as the temperature of the ice is 32 degrees or colder, the ice won’t melt:
    <md>
      T\le 32
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    A line of math containing <m>\gt</m> ,<m>\ge</m>, <m>\lt</m> or <m>\le</m> is called an <term>inequality</term>.
  </p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Notice Something Neat!</title>
    <p>
      Any statement containing an inequality can be “reversed” as long as you make sure that the “mouth” of the symbol points toward the greater or warmer value.
      <md>
        A \ge 2000 \,\,\text{is the same as}\,\, 2000 \le A
      </md>
      <md>
        T \le 32 \,\,\text{is the same as}\,\, 32 \ge T
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    It’s also possible to have a line containing multiple inequality signs. These are called <term>compound inequalities</term>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, if <m>H</m> represents the temperature in your house (in degrees Fahrenheit), a comfortable temperature might be between 68 and 74. Then we could write:
    <md>
      68 \le H \le 74
    </md>
  </p>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Be Careful!</title>
    <p>
      The symbol <m>\lt</m> means “less than”. When we write something like <m>x\lt 5</m> that means that <m>x</m> cannot be equal to 5. 
      It has to be strictly less than 5.
    </p>
    <p>
      By contrast, the symbol <m>\le</m> means “less than OR equal to”. When we write something like <m>x \le 5</m>, 
      that means <m>x</m> could be less than 5 or it could also be equal to 5.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    Inequalities containing a variable <m>x</m> can be solved using almost exactly the way we solved equations.
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>How to Solve Inequalities</alert>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          If necessary, distribute. 
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add or subtract terms to cancel out all the variables on one side of the equation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Clean up the equation by combining like terms.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add or subtract terms to cancel out all the numbers on the other side of the equation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Clean up the equation by combining like terms.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If necessary, finish isolating the variable using multiplication or division
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                If you multiply or divide by a negative number, flip the inequality symbol
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
         </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the inequality
      <md>
        5x+12 \le 2x
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 1: Subtract <m>2x</m> from both sides to move all the variables to the left:
      <md>
        5x+12 - 2x \le 2x -2x
      </md>
      <md>
        3x+12 \le 0
      </md>
      Subtract 12 from both sides to move all the numbers to the right:
      <md>
        3x+12-12\le 0 -12
      </md>
      <md>
        3x \le -12
      </md>
    Divide both sides by 3:
    <md>
      \frac{3x}{3} \le \frac{-12}{3}
    </md>
    <md>
      x \le -4
    </md>
    In other words, any x-value less than or equal to -4 will make the inequality true.
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution 2: Subtract <m>5x</m> from both sides to move all the variables to the right:
      <md>
        5x+12 - 5x \le 2x-5x
      </md>
      <md>
        12 \le -3x
      </md>
      Divide both sides by -3. Since we’re dividing by a negative number, we need to flip the <m>\le</m> sign.
      <md>
        \frac{12}{-3} \ge \frac{-3x}{-3}
      </md>
      <md>
        - 4 \ge x
      </md>
      Notice that we get exactly the same answer as we got before: Any x-value less than or equal to -4 will make the inequality true.
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s08e01">
    <title>Season 8, Episode 1 Practice Problems</title>
    <p>
      Solve the following inequalities:
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>3x+5 \gt 17</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>-0.8n+6 \le 2</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>-4y + 2 \le 10</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>-3p - 9 \lt 0</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>\frac{1}{2}a - 7 \lt 9</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>\frac{5}{3} q + 2 \ge 11</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>6b + \frac{3}{4} \ge 5</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>7 - 1.2 r \gt 3.6</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>2.5m - 1.5 \gt 4</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>\frac{4}{5} s + \le 12</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>3x+4 \gt 2x -7</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="0.6in">
      <p>
        <m>2.5m-4 \ge 1.5m + 2</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p>
        <m>5y-9 \le 2y + 6</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p>
        <m>3(x+4) \gt 2x + 5</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p>
        <m>\frac{1}{2}a + 3 \lt \frac{3}{4}a+1</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.0in">
      <p>
        <m>-2(2y-3) \le y + 7</m>
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <p>
      Write an inequality for each of the following situations:
    </p>
    
    <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p>
        A child’s height, <m>h</m>, must be at least 57 inches for the child to safely ride in the front seat of a car. 
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p>
        The maximum height, <m>h</m>, of a fighter pilot is 77 inches.
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p>
        The total weight, <m>w</m>, of an elevator’s passengers can be no more than 1,200 pounds.
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p>
        The number of items, <m>n</m>, a shopper can have in the express check-out lane is at most 8. Write this as an inequality.
      </p>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section>  